"toolCall","id":"toolu_01FkVd85KUCRzNNTuhuoz6gZ","name":"xorq_select","arguments":{"catalog_path":".xorq/catalog","on":"farmers_markets","compose":"source.limit(3)"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_013FLPa277Kg8d4kTCGXATV3","name":"xorq_select","arguments":{"catalog_path":".xorq/catalog","on":"census_population","compose":"source.filter(source.NAME == \"United States\").select(\"NAME\", \"POPESTIMATE2025\").limit(1)"}}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"anthropic","model":"claude-haiku-4-5","usage":{"input":14,"output":373,"cacheRead":17813,"cacheWrite":1471,"totalTokens":19671,"cost":{"input":0.000014,"output":0.0018650000000000001,"cacheRead":0.0017813000000000002,"cacheWrite":0.00183875,"total":0.00549905},"cacheWrite1h":0,"reasoning":149},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1786914796564,"responseId":"msg_011Ce77mamrG53yKbbFBXdBP","rawStopReason":"tool_use"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"toolu_01FkVd85KUCRzNNTuhuoz6gZ","toolName":"xorq_select","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\"state_name\",\"state_abbr\",\"farmers_markets\",\"csa_programs\",\"farm_stands\",\"snap_ebt_markets\",\"snap_ebt_pct\",\"organic_vendor_markets\",\"organic_pct\",\"markets_with_website\",\"website_pct\"\n\"California\",\"CA\",647,95,223,100,15.5,114,17.6,246,38.0\n\"New York\",\"NY\",555,156,272,41,7.4,45,8.1,283,51.0\n\"Pennsylvania\",\"PA\",426,102,886,18,4.2,43,10.1,226,53.1\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1786914802118},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"toolu_013FLPa277Kg8d4kTCGXATV3","toolName":"xorq_select","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\"NAME\",\"POPESTIMATE2025\"\n\"United States\",341784857\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1786914802119},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Perfect! I can see:\n- The farmers_markets data has state-level data, and I need to sum the \"farmers_markets\" column\n- The census_population data has a row for \"United States\" with POPESTIMATE2025 = 341,784,857\n\nNow I need to create a composed metric that:\n1. Gets the total farmers markets (sum of farmers_markets column)\n2. Gets the US population (POPESTIMATE2025 from the \"United States\" row)\n3. Calculates farmers_markets / population * 100,000\n\nActually, I could compose this from both tables. Let me check if I can create a build script that joins these two. But wait, the farmers_markets data is state-level, not a single total. So I need to sum the farmers_markets first.\n\nLet me use compose to create a simple metric. Actually, I should create a build script that:\n1. Reads farmers markets, aggregates to get total\n2. Reads census, filters to US, gets POPESTIMATE2025\n3. Joins them and calculates the ratio\n\nLet me create that build script.","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! Now I'll create a build script that joins these two datasets to calculate the metric:"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_01CuaFMTNqukzT9qcPfGowcm","name":"write","arguments":{"path":".xorq/scripts/farmers_markets_per_capita.py","content":"import urllib.request as u\n_o = u.build_opener(); _o.addheaders = [(\"User-Agent\", \"Mozilla/5.0\")]; u.install_opener(_o)\n\nfrom xorq.vendor.ibis.common.collections import FrozenDict\nimport xorq.api as xo\n\nso = FrozenDict({\"User-Agent\": \"Mozilla/5.0\"})\ncon = xo.pandas.connect()\n\n# Read farmers markets data\nfm = xo.deferred_read_csv(\n    \"https://harvestlymarkets.com/data/us-farmers-markets-by-state-2026.csv\",\n    con,\n    storage_options=so\n)\n\n# Read census population data\npop = xo.deferred_read_csv(\n    \"https://gist.githubusercontent.com/hussainsultan/369600f5179b22792a2b4c9ed37de056/raw/NST-EST2025-ALLDATA.csv\",\n    con,\n    storage_options=so\n)\n\n# Aggregate farmers markets to get total\nfm_total = fm.aggregate(total_farmers_markets=fm.farmers_markets.sum()).mutate(k=xo.literal(1))\n\n# Get US population\nus_pop = pop.filter(pop.NAME == \"United States\").select(\"POPESTIMATE2025\").mutate(k=xo.literal(1))\n\n# Join and calculate the metric\nexpr = (\n    